## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests understanding of stack augmentation, a practical pattern for maintaining auxiliary state. It demonstrates the ability to design data structures that support efficient queries while handling edge cases like duplicates and empty states.

## Key Concepts
- Two auxiliary stacks (minStack and maxStack) track running minima and maxima in sync with the main stack
- Each push/pop operation updates all three stacks atomically, preserving O(1) time complexity
- Duplicates are handled naturally since the min/max is recalculated at each step
- Empty stack edge cases must be handled (typically by checking stack length before accessing top)

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinMaxStack {
  constructor() {
    this.stack = [];
    this.minStack = [];
    this.maxStack = [];
  }

  push(x) {
    this.stack.push(x);
    this.minStack.push(
      this.minStack.length ? Math.min(x, this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1]) : x
    );
    this.maxStack.push(
      this.maxStack.length ? Math.max(x, this.maxStack[this.maxStack.length - 1]) : x
    );
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.stack.length === 0) return undefined;
    this.minStack.pop();
    this.maxStack.pop();
    return this.stack.pop();
  }

  top() {
    return this.stack[this.stack.length - 1];
  }

  getMin() {
    return this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1];
  }

  getMax() {
    return this.maxStack[this.maxStack.length - 1];
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a dynamic string structure supporting weighted, prefix-based queries and order-statistics. A Trie augmented with per-node aggregates enables efficient prefix sums and k-th retrieval while handling duplicates via cumulative weights.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node aggregates (subtreeWeight, terminalWeight)
- O(len(word)) time complexity for insert/erase operations
- O(len(prefix)) for prefix-sum queries
- O(len(prefix) + len(target_word)) for kth-word queries
- Duplicate handling via cumulative weight values

## Code Example
```javascript
class WeightedTrie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insertWord(word, w) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[idx]) node.children[idx] = new TrieNode();
      node = node.children[idx];
      node.subtreeWeight += w;
    }
    node.terminalWeight += w;
  }

  eraseWord(word, w) {
    this.insertWord(word, -w);
  }

  queryPrefixSum(prefix) {
    let node = this.traverse(prefix);
    return node ? node.subtreeWeight : 0;
  }

  kthWordWithPrefix(prefix, k) {
    let node = this.traverse(prefix);
    if (!node || node.subtreeWeight < k) return null;
    let word = prefix;
    while (!node.terminalWeight) {
      for (let i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
        if (!node.children[i]) continue;
        if (node.children[i].subtreeWeight >= k) {
          word += String.fromCharCode(97 + i);
          node = node.children[i];
          break;
        }
        k -= node.children[i].subtreeWeight;
      }
    }
    return word;
  }

  traverse(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[idx]) return null;
      node = node.children[idx];
    }
    return node;
  }
}

class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.subtreeWeight = 0;
    this.terminalWeight = 0;
  }
}
```

